---
mm: 1.2
kind: board
name: storage-linux-x86_64
derives-from: generic-linux-x86_64
platform-provider: platform.linux.map platform.linux.defaults
platform-provider: mm.mcu platform.linux.storage.device
platform-provider: mm.sdcard.socket platform.linux.storage.socket
---

# storage-linux-x86_64

The generic hosted x86-64 board with its MCU pointed at the emulated storage
bus: a virtual SD card in SPI mode and a virtual W25Q-family flash on SPI 0,
each kept in an image file, and the card published as mm.sdcard.socket.
Everything else is inherited: the same SDK and the same RTC and file-system
providers. The device map is the SDK's own, platform.linux.defaults, rather
than the generic board's named one, because it reads MM_LINUX_DEVICE_MAP, the
file that names the images.

Rebinding mm.mcu is what puts the chips behind the seam mm.sdcard and
mm.spiflash talk to, so they run unmodified. Selecting this board is the only
way anything pays for the emulation; the GPIO, SPI, I2C, UART, ADC, and PWM
of the Linux host are not reachable on it.
